"Estimates the probability of remaining event-free over time while accounting for right-censored observations.\n\nUse when: Observed units have event times and valid right-censoring indicators. Time-to-event probabilities need comparison without assuming a parametric lifetime distribution.\n\nAvoid when: Censoring is informative without adjustment or competing events require a different estimand.\n\nVisual grammar: Horizontal position encodes time since a defined origin. The nonincreasing step curve encodes estimated survival, with marks for censoring and optional confidence bounds.\n\nBuild: Define the event, time origin, observation window, and censoring rules. Compute risk sets and survival drops at observed event times. Draw the step function with censoring marks, confidence limits, and an at-risk table below.\n\nCheck: Check event/censor flags and tied-time handling are correct. Verify risk counts decline consistently with events and censoring. State the independent-censoring assumption and interval method.\n\nPitfalls: Sparse late risk sets make tail estimates unreliable. Curves may cross meaningfully; prefer separate panels when comparison labels or bands become confusing, and never move survival values to remove crossings.\n\nExample: Estimate synthetic component survival to failure, retaining units still operating at study end as right-censored observations.\n\nAlternatives: ECDF, Confidence band, Fan chart."
